## 1. Refined architecture map (with protocol roles)

### 1.1 MCP layer (your existing backbone)

Per MCP docs, the architecture is:

- **MCP Host**: your AI application / agent runtime that coordinates work [modelcontextprotocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/architecture)
- **MCP Client**: one per MCP server, maintains a stateful JSON‑RPC connection [modelcontextprotocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/architecture)
- **MCP Server**: exposes **tools**, **resources**, and **prompts** to clients [modelcontextprotocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/architecture)

In your system:

- **Host** = your agent engines (Outbound/Inbound) + workflow runners  
- **Clients** = MCP client instances inside those engines  
- **Server** = your existing `mcp_server/` exposing GTM tools (email, ads, GA4, Iterable, etc.) [modelcontextprotocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/architecture)

Key point: MCP is **not** about how you choose models or make decisions; it’s the **tool & context layer** under your agents. [modelcontextprotocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/architecture)
That’s exactly where vSR + Jev plug in: **above** MCP, as the decision/orchestration surface.

***

### 1.2 vLLM Semantic Router (vSR) – decision & routing layer

vSR is designed as:

- A **decision layer** between AI clients and model backends [unite](https://www.unite.ai/what-is-model-context-protocol-mcp/)
- It evaluates each request against an **explicit routing policy**, then:
  - Selects a single model, or
  - Coordinates a bounded multi‑model path [unite](https://www.unite.ai/what-is-model-context-protocol-mcp/)

Typical deployment:

- **Envoy** (or similar proxy) accepts client traffic  
- Envoy invokes the **Semantic Router** via the **External Processing (ExtProc)** protocol  
- Router:
  - Extracts signals (headers, path, body snippets, metadata)
  - Evaluates policy (bands, rules, weights)
  - Selects backend(s) and applies route‑specific behavior [unite](https://www.unite.ai/what-is-model-context-protocol-mcp/)

In your architecture:

- vSR sits **in front of**:
  - Your model endpoints (Anthropic, open‑source via vLLM, etc.)
  - Your **Decision Service** (Jev wrapper)
- Every “LLM call” from agents goes through vSR, which decides:
  - Which model band to use
  - Whether to invoke Jev first/after
  - Whether to require human review or extra guardrails [unite](https://www.unite.ai/what-is-model-context-protocol-mcp/)

***

### 1.3 Jev (TypeSafe AI) – typed decision layer

Jev is a **System One** model built for software decisions, not chat:

- Inputs: application state (message, lead data, config, etc.)  
- Outputs: typed decisions:
  - **Choice**: pick from named categories  
  - **Score**: ordered numeric judgment  
  - **Noul**: yes/no proposition with probability [vllm-sr](https://vllm-sr.ai/docs/intro/)

Patterns from TypeSafe docs that map directly to your GTM agent:

- **Atomic question**: one narrow decision per call (e.g., “Is this a buying signal?”) [vllm-sr](https://vllm-sr.ai/docs/intro/)
- **Confidence‑gated routing**: use answer + confidence to decide whether to automate or escalate [vllm-sr](https://vllm-sr.ai/docs/intro/)
- **Composite scoring**: combine multiple Scores (ICP fit, engagement, recency) into a lead priority [vllm-sr](https://vllm-sr.ai/docs/intro/)
- **Intent routing**: choose between deterministic code, specialist LLM, or human based on Jev’s output [vllm-sr](https://vllm-sr.ai/docs/intro/)

Operationally, you wrap Jev in a **Decision Service** that:

- Defines stable question schemas per use case  
- Logs inputs, outputs, and confidence  
- Exposes simple endpoints to agents/workflows [vllm-sr](https://vllm-sr.ai/docs/intro/)

