### 2.3 MCP Host & Agents (your engines)

Your existing **Outbound** and **Inbound** engines become **MCP Hosts**:

- They:
  - Create **MCP Clients** per server (your GTM MCP server, potentially others later) [modelcontextprotocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/architecture)
  - Use those clients to:
    - Discover tools (`list_tools`)  
    - Invoke tools (`call_tool`) with structured arguments  
    - Read resources (e.g., lead records, campaign configs) [modelcontextprotocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/architecture)

**Enhanced agent loop (per task):**

1. Receive task (e.g., “handle inbound reply for conversation X”)  
2. Call **vSR** (via gateway/Envoy) with task metadata  
3. Get routing plan:
   - Model band  
   - Agent path  
   - Required Jev decisions  
4. For each required decision:
   - Call **Decision Service** (Jev) with minimal state  
   - Apply confidence gates:
     - High confidence → proceed  
     - Low confidence → escalate / re‑ask / use stronger model [vllm-sr](https://vllm-sr.ai/docs/intro/)
5. Use selected model (per vSR) to generate content (drafts, responses, plans)  
6. Optionally re‑run Jev to validate output (quality/risk score)  
7. Invoke MCP tools to execute actions (send email, update CRM, launch ads) [modelcontextprotocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/architecture)
8. Log:
   - vSR route
   - Jev decisions + confidence
   - MCP tool calls + outcomes

This cleanly separates:

- **Decision layer**: vSR + Jev  
- **Tool/context layer**: MCP servers  
- **Orchestration**: your agent engines as MCP Hosts [modelcontextprotocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/architecture)

