## 5. Operational patterns & best practices

### 5.1 Policy management

- Treat **routing policies** and **Jev questions** as versioned config:
  - Store in Git (with reviews)  
  - Deploy via CI/CD alongside code  
  - Tag each request with policy/question versions for traceability [unite](https://www.unite.ai/what-is-model-context-protocol-mcp/)

### 5.2 Observability

Log and expose metrics for:

- vSR:
  - Requests per route_key  
  - Distribution of model bands  
  - Policy hit rates  
- Jev:
  - Decision distributions (label frequencies, score histograms)  
  - Confidence distributions  
  - Automation vs. escalation rates  
- MCP:
  - Tool call volumes, latencies, error rates [modelcontextprotocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/learn/architecture)

Dashboards should answer:

- “What % of inbound replies are auto‑handled vs. human‑reviewed?”  
- “How does lead conversion vary by outbound quality score band?”  
- “What’s the cost per handled conversation by model band?”

### 5.3 Safety & fallbacks

- Always define **fallback paths** for low confidence:
  - Human review queue  
  - Conservative default (e.g., no send, no ads change)  
  - Re‑ask with more context or stronger model [vllm-sr](https://vllm-sr.ai/docs/intro/)
- Use **dry‑run mode** extensively in staging:
  - `MCP_DRY_RUN=true`  
  - `JEV_SIMULATION_MODE=true` (if you add it) to log decisions without enforcing them

